<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sheikh Adi ibn Musafir</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1072–<a href="/years/1162">1162</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> · Also known as Şîxadî, Sharaf al-Din Abu al-Fadl Adi ibn Musafir</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sheikh Adi ibn Musafir was a 12th-century mystic, ascetic and theologian who is the central saint of the Yazidi religion, regarded as its reformer, renewer or founder. Born in the 1070s at Bait Far near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baalbek">Baalbek</a>, in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lebanon">Lebanon</a>, he studied in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a> and settled in the Hakkari mountains before 1111, gathering followers at Lalish. Islamic literature and Western scholarship traditionally describe him as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sufism">Sufi</a> mystic of Islamic background, a framing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yazidis">Yazidis</a> reject; in Yazidi belief he is an earthly incarnation of God. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caste">caste</a> structure of Sheikhs, Pirs and Murids is conventionally traced to his reforms. He died in 1162, and his tomb at Lalish is the holiest site in Yazidism.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sheikh-adi-ibn-musafir.jpg" alt="Sheikh Adi ibn Musafir" width="330" height="247" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lalish.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Yazidism, also called Sharfadin, took shape from a pre-Islamic Kurdish religious substratum that was reorganised and given its lasting form by <b>Sheikh Adi ibn Musafir</b> and his successors in the 12th and 13th centuries.</blockquote><a href="/religion/yazidism">Yazidism · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>…Ali began to preach the doctrine of the Druze faith in Cairo in 1017; in the 12th century <b>Sheikh Adi ibn Musafir</b> settled in the valley of Lalish, united the scattered Yazidi tribes and gave Yazidism its lasting form; Guru Nanak began his mission after an experience at…</blockquote><a href="/essays/faiths-on-the-move">Faiths on the Move</a></li></ul></section>
